@page "/docs/tools/vs-code"

@* IzzyBlazor for VS Code, written from the extension itself (0.9.4, Nov 26, 2026: its readme, package.json and
   out/*.js; the extension is in no repo on this machine). The first of five pages in the sidebar group "VS Code":
   install, what it needs, the side bar, review before a change, what each account gets, settings, keys and
   Check Setup (out/doctor.js). The plans table repeats pricing's rows (Components/Pages/Pricing.razor) and the
   overview's "What each needs": keep the three in step. *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="vs-code">
    <Lead>
        The IzzyBlazor extension puts the catalog, the component reference, a properties panel, a live preview
        and the accessibility audit in VS Code. It runs the <Code>izzy</Code> command for everything it
        does, so the editor writes the files a terminal would.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Install</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In VS Code, open Extensions, search for IzzyBlazor and choose Install. Or run this in a terminal:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">code --install-extension izzyblazor.izzyblazor</Code>
        <TextBlock>The extension needs VS Code 1.101 or later (1.106 to put Properties on the right), and:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The .NET 10 SDK or later.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The <Code>izzy</Code> tool. When it's missing or too old, the extension offers to install it. When the app has its own copy of izzy in <Code>.config/dotnet-tools.json</Code>, the extension runs that copy, so izzy's version matches the app. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/cli">The izzy CLI</Anchor>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Chrome or Edge, for the accessibility audit only.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A trusted folder. You can browse the catalog in any folder. A command that runs izzy on your app or changes its files needs a folder you trust.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            In WSL, over SSH, in a dev container and in Codespaces, izzy installs and runs where the project is.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">First steps</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Open the IzzyBlazor icon in the Activity Bar. Get Started has five steps: install izzy, create an app
            or set up one you have, sign in, add a block or a component, and set up your coding agent. Run
            IzzyBlazor: Get Started in the Command Palette to open it again.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The side bar</Heading>
        <Table Items="Views" Caption="The views in the IzzyBlazor side bar" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="View" Width="Length.Rem(10)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it has">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Read"><Anchor Href="@context!.Href">@context.Link</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The status bar shows your plan: Free, Pro preview or Pro. Click it to sign in, sign out or open your
            account. A right-click in a <Code>.razor</Code> file or on a file in the Explorer has one IzzyBlazor
            menu with the commands for that place.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">You see a change first</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A command that adds or changes files in your app, such as a block, a new page or setting up
            IzzyBlazor, asks izzy what it would change and opens the answer in VS Code's Refactor Preview:
            each file with a checkbox and its diff. Nothing is written until you apply it, and undo takes it
            back. When a file has unsaved changes, the extension asks you to save it first. When a file is
            already there with other content, you choose to keep yours or to see the replacement as a change.
            After you apply, the extension offers to build the app, and a failed build's errors are in the
            Problems panel.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A component's tag or example, and an option changed in Properties, go straight into the open editor,
            where undo takes them back. New App and Write Agent Files list what they would write and ask before
            izzy writes it.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The extension applies a reviewed change itself and adds the item's packages after. It doesn't
            record the item in <Code>.izzy/items.json</Code>, as the same command in a terminal does.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Copy izzy Command, on an item, gives you the same command for a terminal. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What each account gets</Heading>
        <Table Items="Plans" Caption="The extension by account" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="In the editor">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="No account">@context!.Open</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="A free account">@context!.Free</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Pro">@context!.Pro</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Sign In shows a code. Compare it with the code in your browser, then approve. A command your account
            doesn't have stays in its menu and says what it needs when you choose it. Pro preview is Pro's
            tools at no charge for a reserved founding price, until Pro is on sale. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/account">Your account</Anchor> and <Anchor Href="pricing">Pricing</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Coding agents</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In a trusted folder, the extension registers <Code>izzy mcp</Code> with VS Code. With a free
            account, agent mode can then list, describe and add the items your account has, with no settings
            file. Write Agent Files runs <Code>izzy agent</Code> in your app for
            other agents. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/ai-assistants">AI assistants</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Settings</Heading>
        <Table Items="Settings" Caption="The extension's settings" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Setting" Width="Length.Rem(20)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Default" Width="Length.Rem(6)"><code>@context!.Default</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">Keyboard shortcuts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Each works in a <Code>.razor</Code> file. On a Mac, press Cmd for Ctrl and Option for Alt.</TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Keys" Caption="The extension's keyboard shortcuts" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Keys" Width="Length.Rem(10)"><Kbd Keys="@context!.Name" /></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Command">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">When something doesn't work</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Run IzzyBlazor: Check Setup, also under Help and Feedback. It checks the .NET SDK, izzy and its version
            against your app's package, the app's own copy of izzy, your account, the browser for the audit,
            whether the folder is trusted, and VS Code's version. Each line says what it found and offers the fix. Report a Bug fills in
            the result and your versions. Show Log opens what izzy printed.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

